---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Challenges with WebAssembly — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="The same Rust code runs in a browser tab. Getting it to compile was the easy part. Getting it to paint the right colours, read files reliably and ship without breaking took much longer."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-26">September 26, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Challenges with WebAssembly</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          The same Rust code runs in a browser tab. Getting it to compile was
          the easy part. Getting it to paint the right colours, read files
          reliably and ship without breaking took much longer.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 7 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          The <a href="/app.html">web version</a> of LightPhotos is the
          desktop app's Rust code compiled to WebAssembly, drawing with WebGPU
          and opening your folder through the browser's File System Access
          API. It works in desktop Chrome and Edge.
        </p>
        <p>
          Almost none of the hard bugs showed up at compile time. The browser
          has no clock the standard library can use, no threads without extra
          setup, no way to recover from a crash, a screen that won't do colour
          conversion for you, and a file API that throws errors under load.
          Each one needed its own workaround, and a few of them changed how
          the desktop app works too.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>"It compiles" tells you almost nothing.</strong> The first
          time the app ran in a browser it hit three bugs, and not one of them
          could have been caught by the compiler.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write down your predictions, then check them.</strong> My
          plan got memory right and threading completely wrong. I only know
          that because I wrote the plan down first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Without crash recovery, every assert in every library is
          your problem.</strong> In the browser a panic kills the worker, so
          the app has to check a file before handing it to a decoder that
          might panic.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How you deploy is part of the program.</strong> Two files
          from the same build cached for different lengths of time broke
          every photo for anyone who had visited recently.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The shape of it</h2>
      <p>
        The browser build is two programs. The main one runs the window, the
        interface and the GPU on the page's main thread. A second, smaller one
        runs inside Web Workers and does the slow work: decoding RAW files and
        rendering exports. It exists because a worker has no window, so it
        can't run the interface or the GPU code at all.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 214" role="img" aria-label="The browser build. The page's main thread runs the interface and draws with WebGPU. It reads and writes your folder through the File System Access API. It sends decode and export jobs to up to four Web Workers, each a separate copy of a smaller WebAssembly program with its own memory. Downloads: about 3.2 MB for the main program and 1.1 MB for the worker, compressed.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">ONE BROWSER TAB</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="28" width="250" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="50">Main thread</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="68">interface, catalog, WebGPU drawing</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="86">12 MB wasm, 3.2 MB compressed</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="250" y1="64" x2="300" y2="64"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M300 59 L310 64 L300 69 Z"></path>
