        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Everything is built with trunk, which runs the Rust compiler and
        produces the JavaScript glue. Even that had a catch. One setting the
        GPU code needs is supposed to go in trunk's config file, but in the
        version I use it never reaches the compiler, so every build script
        sets it as an environment variable instead.
      </p>

      <h2>It compiled. It didn't run.</h2>
      <p>
        Getting the code to compile for the browser at all turned up the
        expected list: no system trash, no clipboard, no random-number source
        until you tell a library to use the browser's, and thread spawns that
        fail. Those are compile-time or startup problems, and they're
        straightforward.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I opened it in a browser, and it crashed.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Asking for the time panics.</strong> Rust's standard
          <code>Instant::now()</code> isn't implemented in the browser, and it
          doesn't return an error. It crashes. The app checks the clock at
          startup, so every launch died. Every timer in the app now uses a
          small library that asks the browser's clock instead, and so does a
          patched copy of the RAW decoder.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You can't wait on the main thread.</strong> The desktop app
          waits for the GPU to be ready before showing a window. A browser
          page must hand control back to the browser, or nothing ever
          happens. GPU setup now starts in the background and the app checks
          each frame whether it's done.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The window starts at zero by zero.</strong> Until the
          browser reports the canvas size, it's 0&times;0 pixels, and WebGPU
          rejects every drawing command outside the canvas. On a high-DPI
          screen the clipping rectangles were also twice too big.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>WebGPU is stricter than Metal.</strong> One shader stopped
          early for some pixels and then sampled a texture. Metal and Vulkan
          had quietly allowed it. WebGPU's validator refused to compile it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The colours were wrong</h2>
      <p>
        Once it drew, everything looked muddy. A 50% gray showed up as about
        21%.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the desktop, the app hands the GPU colours in linear light, and the
        screen converts them to the sRGB values a monitor expects. A WebGPU
        canvas never offers that conversion. The linear values went straight
        to the screen, so every midtone came out far too dark. Gray test bars
        that should have read 64, 128 and 192 read 13, 55 and 134.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fix was to do the conversion in the shaders ourselves, on every
        platform, not just the browser. With one path, the desktop and
        browser builds can no longer drift apart on colour.
      </p>

      <h2>Threads that aren't threads</h2>
      <p>
        Before starting the port I wrote a feasibility memo. For threading,
        it predicted the standard approach: shared memory between workers,
        which needs the site to send two special security headers, plus a
        library that lets Rust's thread pool run in the browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        That library turned out not to fit. It expects JavaScript to set up
        the thread pool before the Rust program starts, and this app is a
        normal Rust program with its own <code>main()</code>. So I dropped
        shared memory altogether. Each worker is a separate copy of the
        worker program. The main thread sends it a file's bytes and gets
        pixels back.
      </p>
      <p>
        That turned out simpler than the plan. It needs no special headers in
        production and no experimental Rust. The cost is memory, because each
        worker has its own heap. So the pool stops at four workers, however
        many cores you have.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same memo's warnings about memory were right. A browser tab gets
        a 32-bit address space, and memory it grows never shrinks back. I've
        written about that in <a
        href="/blogs/measuring-a-gui-app.html">measuring a GUI app</a> and
        <a href="/blogs/never-load-the-whole-folder.html">never load the
        whole folder</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>No second chances after a panic</h2>
      <p>
        On the desktop, if the RAW decoder panics on a strange file, the app
        catches it and shows a placeholder. The browser build can't do that.
        WebAssembly there has no stack unwinding, so a panic kills the worker
        outright.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the app checks a file before handing it to the decoder:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>Layouts the decoder would hit a <code>todo!()</code> on are turned away.</li>
        <li>Fujifilm's X-Trans sensors, which hit an <code>unreachable!()</code>, are turned away.</li>
        <li>
          Image dimensions are checked for overflow, because on a 32-bit
          target a big RAW file's width times height can overflow where it
          never would on a 64-bit Mac.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Where it can, the worker also avoids the decoder completely by using
        the JPEG preview most cameras embed in the RAW file. It's faster, and
        it can't crash.
      </p>

      <h2>Files, one handle at a time</h2>
      <p>
        The File System Access API is what makes this possible at all: you
        pick a folder, and the page can read and write inside it. But a
        folder there has no path, only handles, and every operation is
        asynchronous. Code that used to be "list the directory" became "ask
        for the list, and apply it when it arrives".
      </p>
      <p>
        Two errors took the longest to understand.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong><code>NotReadableError</code>.</strong> Chrome throws it
          when too many file reads run at once. Two at a time never tripped
          it. The app allows four and retries with random, growing delays.
          Retrying immediately, or at fixed intervals, failed again because
          all the reads retried at the same moment.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>RangeError: Array buffer allocation failed</code>.</strong>
          Reading a RAW file copied it once into JavaScript and again into
          WebAssembly memory. A few of those in a row and the tab ran out of
          room. Now the bytes move across without the extra copy.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        There are rougher edges I haven't fixed. You pick the folder again
        every time you open the page. Deleting a photo is permanent, because
        the API has no trash. The app says so before it deletes.
      </p>

      <h2>Deploying broke it</h2>
      <p>
        The worst bug of the port wasn't in the Rust code at all. The site is
        served by Cloudflare, which cached the worker's JavaScript for four
        hours and its WebAssembly file for zero. After a release, anyone who
        had visited recently got old JavaScript paired with new WebAssembly.
        Every decode failed with an error saying a function didn't exist.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fix: the worker files are now requested with the main bundle's
        build hash stuck on the end of the address, so a new release always
        means new URLs for both. Moving the app under <code>/app/</code> broke
        the worker's address too. It now works out where it lives from the
        page instead of assuming.
      </p>

      <h2>What the browser version gives up</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>Only desktop Chrome and Edge, which have WebGPU and folder access.</li>
        <li>No HEIC, no face detection, no subject selection.</li>
        <li>No Immich upload, because Immich servers don't allow requests from web pages.</li>
        <li>No clipboard, no Lightroom preset import, no choosing where exports go. They go to an <code>Exports</code> folder inside the one you picked.</li>
        <li>A download of about 4.3&nbsp;MB compressed before the app starts.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Was it worth it?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. Being able to send someone a link instead of an installer is
        worth a lot, and the port made the desktop app better along the way:
        one colour path everywhere, thread pools that don't panic when they
        can't start, and a thumbnail cache that lives next to your photos.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you try the <a href="/app.html">web app</a> and it breaks, I'd like
        to know which browser and what you were doing. The <a
        href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/issues">issue
        tracker</a> is the best place.
      </p>

